          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em well-order
            on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            that allows one to interpret
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            as the relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            on the ordinal numbers less than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            (although the more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relations"} well-founded relations
            also allow this).
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering+theorem"} well-ordering theorem
            states precisely that every set may be equipped with a well-order. This theorem follows from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a
            %strong well-order
            if it is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitive
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
            = succeed "," do
              %strong well-ordered set
            = succeed "\u2019)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
            = succeed "." do
              %strong woset
          %p
            Other definitions of a well-order may be found in the literature; they are equivalent given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %ul
            %li
              a well-order is precisely a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-order"} pseudo-order
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≺
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            %li
              a well-order is precisely a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} total order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⪯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \preceq
              whose strict version
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              (defined by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec y
              iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⪯
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \preceq y
              and
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne y
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
            %li
              (assuming also
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              with no infinite descending sequence
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ≺
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ≺
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≺
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \prec x_2 \prec x_1 \prec x_0
            %li
              (assuming also
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⪯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \preceq
              such that every infinite descending sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ⪯
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⪯
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⪯
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \preceq x_2 \preceq x_1 \preceq x_0
              has
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i = x_{i^+}
              for some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              (and hence for infinitely many
              = succeed ");" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %li
              a well-order on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is precisely a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-order"} pseudo-order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              with the property that every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+subset"} inhabited subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              has a least element (an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot_U
              such that no
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
              satisfies
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec \bot_U
            %li
              a well-order on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is precisely a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} total order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⪯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \preceq
              with the property that every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+subset"} inhabited subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              has a least element (an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot_U
              such that every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
              satisfies
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mi U
                      %mo ⪯
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot_U \preceq x
          %p
            The really interesting thing here is that every well-order is a pseudo-order; it is a constructive theorem that every pseudo-order is weakly extensional (and so extensional if well-founded) and transitive. (For a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+counterexample"} weak counterexample
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≺
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec y
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            is true and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is false; this is a well-order that's a pseudo-order iff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            holds.) For the other equivalences, we're simply using well-known classical equivalents for well-foundedness and the classical correspondence between a pseudo-order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            and its
            %span.newWikiWord
              reflexive closure
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/reflexive+closure"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⪯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \preceq
          %p
            For reference, a
            %strong classical well-order
            is any order satisfying the last definition (a total order that is classically well-founded). A classically well-ordered set is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+set"} choice set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denial+inequality"} unequal
            elements has a classical well-order, then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            follows. (But there are still interesting examples of classically well-ordered sets in constructive mathematics, such as the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Higgs set
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Higgs+object"} ?
          %h3#wellorders_are_pseudoorders Well-orders are pseudo-orders
          %p
            As stated above, well-founded extensional transitive relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are pseudo-orders, assuming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times X
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lexicographic+order"} lexicographically
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≺
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b) \prec (a', b')
              if either
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \prec a'
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a'
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \prec b
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subset X \times X
              be the set of pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y)
              of non-equal elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              that are incomparable in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is inhabited. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              has a minimal element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b)
              (using excluded middle). Then, for every
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⪯
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \preceq x
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⪯
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \preceq a
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \prec b
              follows by transitivity, contradiction. Hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec a
              for every
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
              be minimal such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⪯
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec a' \preceq a
              for every
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec b
            %p Claim:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x: x \prec a'\} = \{x: x \prec b\}.
            %p
              We know already the right side is contained in the left. In the other direction, suppose
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec a'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b)
              was chosen minimal in the lexicographic order,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              are comparable. If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⪯
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \preceq x \prec a'
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec b
            %p
              But now, by extensionality,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a' = b
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⪯
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \preceq a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              was empty, so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+relation"} connected
              and therefore (being already transitive and irreflexive and using excluded middle again) a pseudo-order.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-ordered+set"} pseudo-ordered set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo &lt;
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1 \lt \cdots \lt x_n\}
                is well-ordered.
            %li
              %p
                The set of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                is well-ordered under the usual order
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo &lt;
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            %li
              %p
                More generally, any set of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+numbers"} ordinal numbers
                (or even the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
                of all ordinal numbers) is well-ordered under the usual order
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &lt;
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
                (which, constructively, may not be a pseudo-order).
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+numbers"} cardinal numbers
                of well-orderable sets (the well-orderable cardinals), forming a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
                of the ordinals, are well-ordered. So by the well-ordering theorem, the class of
                %em all
                cardinal numbers is well-ordered.
            %li
              %p
                A special case of the well-ordering theorem is the existence of a well-order on the set of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
          %h2#interpretation_as_an_ordinal_number Interpretation as an ordinal number
          %p
            Any well-ordered set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            defines an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and an order isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and the set of ordinal numbers less than
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            may be identified (up to isomorphism of wosets) with the von Neumann ordinal
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            itself (if any) is mapped to the ordinal number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \setminus \{\bot\}
            (if any) is mapped to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (if any) is mapped to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p
            This may be defined immediately (and constructively) as a recursively defined function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to the class of all ordinal numbers:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi sup
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi x
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                  %mo ;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(x) = \sup_{t \prec x} r(t)^+ ;
          %p
            the validity of this sort of recursive definition is precisely what the well-foundedness of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            allows. Here,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi β
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta^+
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
            of the ordinal number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sup
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sup
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
            operation on ordinal numbers (which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
            of von Neumann ordinals).
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a set, the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            in the class of all ordinals is also a set (by the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            is an order isomorphism between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and the set of ordinals less than the next ordinal,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} im
                    %mi r
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \coloneqq (\im r)^+
          %h2#simulations Simulations
          %p
            Given two well-ordered sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon S \to T
            is a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simulation"} simulation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            if
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) \prec f(y)
              whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \prec y
              and
            %li
              given
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \prec f(x)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \prec x
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = f(y)
          %p
            Note that any simulation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            must be unique. Thus, well-ordered sets and simulations form a category that is in fact a (large)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            is in fact the poset of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+numbers"} ordinal numbers
          %h2#successor Successor
          %p
            A well-ordered set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            comes equipped with a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi succ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  succ\colon S \to S
                  \\,
            that sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in S
            to the lowest element of the subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_a := \{ s \in S, a \prec s\}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong limit
              well-order is a well-order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              whose successor map is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+function"} total function
          %p
            Similarly, one may define a successor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of well-ordered sets, taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to the well-order obtained by freely adjoining a (new) top element to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-quasi-order"} well-quasi-order
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/marked+extensional+well-founded+relation"} marked extensional well-founded relation
        .revisedby
